#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e38090-95df-5db7-8495-6bea995248d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277722} {:answer/id #uuid "f996bccc-b3d3-5796-bcf0-cb628bc10d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277723} {:answer/id #uuid "66da0f38-31fa-5f51-8bd1-0b5e6a0fe3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277724} {:answer/id #uuid "aee4d362-b9eb-5fb6-99a1-bd156728dc9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277725} {:answer/id #uuid "30e87dee-e647-52cd-9da8-56fc054e7e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f996bccc-b3d3-5796-bcf0-cb628bc10d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277723} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bcc09e5-bdca-52ca-a12b-94e93877a51f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaa1873e-b871-5487-ad2a-55300889eb98" :question/math-academy-id "q-124707" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{530}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{530}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{530}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-170$\n\n- $-{530}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={190}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${190}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{530}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977500/assets/1e741939f538fc5dfba9b5ff6f3b4ce0e68c5ebd746dfae533d3209c0c29feba.png)\n\nWe see that ${190}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant III. Therefore, $-{530}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d3afba9-fd91-5793-aee8-3718f16f14f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277729} {:answer/id #uuid "23c60861-0323-5edb-acec-b21d51321b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277730} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7ba99-a50a-59f8-be62-1376c3f6f614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277731} {:answer/id #uuid "afc08a3c-efbb-5077-adfa-ca8afaf5cbf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277732} {:answer/id #uuid "de4cd345-bf42-56df-a44e-cc43b61b90ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c60861-0323-5edb-acec-b21d51321b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277730} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8cd84e6-a4ce-5bbc-a7ae-323b44b80f19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aba695cd-659a-5af1-80a1-f8970be8eb8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-22979" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-{95}^{∘}?$\n\n- ${625}^{∘}$\n- ${160}^{∘}$\n- $-{455}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}:$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{95}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={265}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={625}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than ${625}^{∘}.$ So we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{95}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{455}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only ${625}^{∘}$ and $-{455}^{∘}$ are coterminal with $-{95}^{∘}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3700d87-3443-5005-9334-e439aa751883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277736} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7fc5dc-1382-507a-bee9-299374e56b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277737} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d8d0cf-f4a1-5fcb-9199-69656fc5b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277738} {:answer/id #uuid "71ea9b5e-2d12-5e12-8273-4c5575fd34b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277739} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4469c6-d075-5d73-baad-25f557da8189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c7fc5dc-1382-507a-bee9-299374e56b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277737} :answer-field/id #uuid "f379d197-7992-501b-88af-92f31e188315" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72a396d7-e8cf-5458-9d0a-c10ca03427ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-22984" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n- $4\\pi $\n- $5\\pi $\n- $-2\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\pi =4\\pi \\,✓$\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\cdot (2\\pi )=6\\pi $\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $6\\pi .$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\pi =0$\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-2\\pi \\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ are coterminal with $2\\pi .$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abf99351-d559-589a-9a37-a1600baf1016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057850} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d9f1fe-6067-5180-a32d-4b4930a7d48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057851} {:answer/id #uuid "d787b8a2-5827-56ab-a0f9-76f47a18d9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057852} {:answer/id #uuid "221b775d-5ee9-5c20-aa38-f4e13f9cdfed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057853} {:answer/id #uuid "5f41b252-c02e-51d3-9058-240933e9bd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221b775d-5ee9-5c20-aa38-f4e13f9cdfed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057853} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b77333-ee8c-506c-bb0a-827788d71f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7636651-ac27-54dd-bac3-a58cbd0f8a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-23191" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\n- $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n- $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$\n- $-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1000f1-7874-54ec-a25c-a6fdd4c99c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058130} {:answer/id #uuid "3d05c2d3-5f8b-520c-9698-93cd44016078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058131} {:answer/id #uuid "3e675e47-ccc8-5f07-88dd-4486ea14690b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058132} {:answer/id #uuid "4077c45f-7284-5f8a-b112-9435133409d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058133} {:answer/id #uuid "73d4409a-0928-587b-a8b0-c3b59584d36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e675e47-ccc8-5f07-88dd-4486ea14690b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058132} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6c658f-3e45-5acf-b1fd-cc6fc99efea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f5801f-10e6-5373-bb84-2300dad1ed58" :question/math-academy-id "q-34403" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977500/assets/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant."}]}
